meta {
  name: 05 Center provisions while blocked
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/centers/me/students
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "firstName": "Blocked",
    "lastName": "Attempt",
    "email": "blocked-attempt@example.com",
    "phone": "+237690000001"
  }
}

docs {
  Uses the CENTER token ({{accessToken}}), not the student one.

  A blocked center cannot grant new access. Provisioning a student and minting
  an activation key are the only two center routes that stop.

  - entitled center -> 201
  - blocked center  -> 403 SUBSCRIPTION_INACTIVE, `subscriptionStatus: BLOCKED`

  The same applies to `Center Students / 05 Issue activation key`: a key IS
  access, so re-issuing one to a blocked center would hand out exactly what the
  block withholds.

  ## The state that must NOT refuse: TRIAL_PENDING

  Register a brand-new center and try this BEFORE any student activates.

  It must return 201.

  That center has `studentsMayLearn: false` — the trial has not started, because
  the clock starts at the first activation. If provisioning followed that flag,
  a new center could never create the student whose activation starts its
  trial, and onboarding would deadlock on itself for every customer. So
  provisioning has its own rule: everything except BLOCKED is allowed.

  This is the single easiest thing to break later by "simplifying" the two
  checks into one. `06 Center dashboard while blocked` and the test file
  `test/center-blocked-surface.spec.ts` both exist to catch it.
}
